# KNOWN GAP (Rust gg lane; the SELF-HOST lane COMPILES AND RUNS both shapes
# correctly — a succession milestone, not a regression).
#
# A `Vector.iter()` trait-equip chain that appears ONLY inside certain
# syntactic positions is REJECTED outright:
#
#     error[E_NoMethodFound]: no method `iter` found on type `Vector[int]`
#
# and the diagnostic's span points at `<std.collections>:26:1`, a blank line.
#
# ORDER-DEPENDENT, therefore a defect and not a rule: adding ONE unrelated
# earlier `v.iter().any(...)` anywhere in the program makes the IDENTICAL
# expression compile. Acceptance that depends on program order is not sanctioned
# by anything in `docs/language-design.md` or `docs/book/`.
#
# The affected positions measured so far: f-string interpolation · assert
# condition · dict-literal value · match-expression arm · list-comprehension
# body · `as`-cast operand · `is`-pattern operand. Two of them are in this
# fixture (list-comprehension body, `is`-pattern operand); they are the two the
# self-host lane also still fails on for SEPARATE, distinct reasons (a
# comprehension mangling corruption that truncates the receiver prefix to
# `int64_t__any`, and an `Option__int64_t`-from-`int` typing error), so this one
# file pins both lanes' work on the same two shapes.
#
# ROOT CAUSE NOT LOCATED. It is NOT `apply_inferred_method_targs`
# (`Expr::StringLiteral(_, interp_exprs)` IS handled there) — it is a
# typecheck-time defect upstream of that pass.
#
# GRADUATES to a live top-level fixture when a cold trait-equip chain is
# accepted at every position.

from std.collections import Vector

bool gt_five(int x):
    return x > 5

int main():
    Vector[int] v = Vector[int]()
    v.push(3)
    v.push(9)

    # POSITION: list-comprehension body (chain appears nowhere else)
    Vector[bool] xs = [v.iter().any(gt_five) for i in 0..1]
    print(xs.get(0).unwrap())

    # POSITION: `is`-pattern operand
    bool found = v.iter().find(gt_five) is Some(_)
    print(found)
    return 0
